        ("刚挤出地铁，一抬头，通道尽头的广告牌亮着暖橙色的光。 ——“家的味道”。 （眼前忽然恍惚） 我好像回到了那个永远泛着油烟的黄昏厨房。我的个子那么小，只够得着桌沿，看妈妈切西红柿。她动作快，汁液溅开，像晚霞染红了她的手指。我偷偷伸手去拿，被她用手背轻轻一拍：“小馋猫。”我缩回手，指尖还留着一点凉。可下一秒，那块最大、最红的，就被送到了我嘴边。 糖分和酸味在舌尖炸开。世界简单得像那块西红柿，甜得直接，没有潜台词。那时的我只有一个身份，一个名字，一张不用思考就能戴稳的脸——她的孩子。", "Squeezing out of the subway, I look up. At the end of the passage, a billboard glows warm orange: \"A Taste of Home.\"\n(Everything blurs for a moment.)\nI am back in that dusk-lit kitchen that always smelled of cooking oil. I was so small I could barely reach the edge of the table, watching Mom slice tomatoes. She worked fast, and the juice splashed like a sunset staining her fingers. I sneaked a hand toward the plate, and she gave it a light tap with the back of her hand: \"Little glutton.\" I pulled back, a bit of coolness still on my fingertips. But the next second, the biggest, reddest slice was right at my lips.\nSweet and sour burst on my tongue. The world was as simple as that tomato, plainly sweet, with nothing left unsaid. Back then I had only one identity, one name, one face I could wear without thinking: her child."),
        ("红灯，车堵着。路边的中学正好放学，一群穿着肥大校服的学生说笑着涌过斑马线。 有个女孩皱着眉，还在看书。 （胸口忽然被什么撞了一下） 那年的期末考，教室里的风扇也是这样，把光斑切割得支离破碎。同桌悄悄用胳膊肘碰我，一张写着“第三题答案”的小纸条推到我手边。我摇摇头，把试卷往自己胳膊底下藏了藏。 讲台上，班主任赞许地对我点点头。 她不知道，我摇头不是因为诚实，只是……不想让她超过我。 那天，我给自己戴上了第一张真正意义上的面具——“诚实的好学生”。我学会了，原来在某些标准里，善意的伪装是被允许的。", "A red light, traffic backed up. The middle school by the road has just let out, and a crowd of students in baggy uniforms laughs its way across the crosswalk.\nOne girl, frowning, is still reading.\n(Something suddenly knocks against my chest.)\nAt that year's final exam, the classroom fan chopped the sunlight into broken pieces just like this. My deskmate nudged me with her elbow and slid over a note that said \"Answer to Question 3.\" I shook my head and tucked my test paper under my arm.\nAt the front of the room, our homeroom teacher gave me an approving nod.\nShe didn't know I shook my head not out of honesty, but because... I didn't want my deskmate to beat me.\nThat day I put on my first real mask: \"the honest good student.\" I learned that by some standards, a well-meant disguise is allowed."),
        ("便利店门口，一对年轻情侣互相喂冰淇淋，笑得很轻，但眼睛很亮。 我低下头，把耳机声音调大，却好像什么都听不见了。 （心脏某个角落，细微地抽动了一下） 你说我安静，和你在一起的时候。 那是在末班地铁上吧，车厢空荡荡的，玻璃上倒映着我们依偎的影子。你的肩膀很稳。我笑着靠过去，什么也没说。 可我心里在翻江倒海。我在想，刚才那句话会不会太蠢，这个笑是不是不够好看，我是不是……像你喜欢的那个样子？ 那份安静，是我最柔软的伪装。那张叫“恋人”的面具，薄得像皮肤，服帖到有时候，我甚至忘了自己戴着它。", "Outside a convenience store, a young couple feed each other ice cream, laughing softly, their eyes bright.\nI lower my head and turn up my earphones, but it's as if I can't hear anything at all.\n(Some corner of my heart gives a tiny twitch.)\nYou said I was quiet when I was with you.\nIt was on the last train, I think. The car was empty, and the window reflected the two of us leaning together. Your shoulder was steady. I smiled, leaned in, and said nothing.\nBut inside I was in turmoil. Was what I just said too stupid? Was this smile pretty enough? Was I... the way you liked me to be?\nThat quiet was my softest disguise. The mask called \"lover\" was as thin as skin, and it fit so well that sometimes I even forgot I was wearing it."),
        ("到家前最后一个路口，等绿灯。对面写字楼还零星亮着几层灯，像巨大而沉默的蜂巢。 其中一格，像极了我曾经的工位。 （手指下意识地在方向盘上，敲出打字的节奏） 凌晨两点，咖啡早就凉了，喉咙里只有苦涩。屏幕右下角的聊天框弹出来，老板的头像在跳：“方案很棒！但能不能再优化三个版本？” 我的手指比脑子快。 “当然，马上！” 发送。 黑色屏幕的倒影里，映出我自己的脸——嘴角上翘的弧度，和昨天对客户说“合作愉快”时，分毫不差。熟练得令人心惊。", "The last intersection before home, waiting for the light to turn green. A few floors of the office tower across the street are still lit, like a huge, silent hive.\nOne of those cells looks just like my old desk.\n(My fingers tap out a typing rhythm on the steering wheel without thinking.)\nTwo in the morning. The coffee had long gone cold, and only bitterness was left in my throat. A chat window popped up in the corner of the screen, my boss's avatar bouncing: \"Great proposal! But could you optimize three more versions?\"\nMy fingers moved faster than my mind.\n\"Of course, right away!\"\nSend.\nIn the black screen's reflection was my own face, the corners of my mouth turned up at exactly the same angle as when I told a client \"Pleasure working with you\" the day before. So practiced it was frightening."),
        ("天阴沉下来，雨刮器自己动了一下。马上就要到幼儿园那条街了。 手机一震，是老师发来的照片：女儿午睡时，手里还攥着一片饼干。 （那股熟悉的、夹杂着焦虑和温柔的情绪，像潮水一样漫上来） 那天也是这样，突然就下雨了。她在幼儿园门口，指着路边小贩手里的气球，哭得上气不接下气：“我要！我要！” 我来不及撑伞，蹲下来，用拇指擦她湿漉漉的小脸，声音轻得自己都陌生：“乖，不哭，妈妈下班第一个来接你，给你买。” 她立刻就笑了，眼泪还挂在睫毛上。 我站起身，雨点打在脸上。一股巨大的恍惚感击中了我——刚才那个用最温柔的声音许诺的人，和两个小时前，在会议室里冷着脸说“这个提案不行”的人，真的是同一个我吗？ 雨滴是冷的。我抱紧怀里这个温暖的小身体，像抱住一个快要消失的凭证。", "The sky darkens, and the wipers twitch on their own. The kindergarten street is just ahead.\nMy phone buzzes. It's a photo from the teacher: my daughter napping, still clutching a piece of cookie.\n(That familiar mix of worry and tenderness rises like a tide.)\nIt was like this that day too, rain out of nowhere. At the kindergarten gate she pointed at the balloons in a street vendor's hand, sobbing so hard she could barely breathe: \"I want one! I want one!\"\nThere was no time to open my umbrella. I crouched down and wiped her wet little face with my thumb, my voice so soft it sounded strange even to me: \"Be good, don't cry. Mommy will come get you first thing after work and buy you one.\"\nShe smiled right away, tears still on her lashes.\nI stood up, rain hitting my face, and a huge sense of unreality struck me. The one who just made that promise in the gentlest voice, and the one who coldly said \"This proposal won't work\" in a meeting room two hours ago, were they really the same me?\nThe raindrops were cold. I held the warm little body in my arms tight, like holding on to proof that was about to disappear."),
